# Environment for the API process. ADR-0059, ADR-0050, ADR-0040, ADR-0027.
#
# ============================================================================
# NOTHING LOADS THIS FILE FOR YOU. EXPORT THESE, OR NAME THE FILE ON THE COMMAND LINE.
# ============================================================================
#
# Corrected 2026-08-16 (F-204). This header used to say "copy to `apps/api/.env` before
# `pnpm dev`", and both halves were fiction: no workspace declares a `dev` script except
# `apps/web`, `apps/api` has no `dotenv` dependency, no script passes `--env-file`, and
# Node does not auto-load a `.env`. A developer whose boot had just refused would have
# copied the file, re-run, got the identical refusal, and believed the variables were set.
#
#   export BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001
#
# — or, to use a copy of this file as written, name it explicitly, which Node does support:
#
#   cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env
#   node --env-file=apps/api/.env apps/api/dist/main.js
#
# It is NOT read by Docker Compose: the repository-root `.env.example` is Compose's
# interpolation source and holds the role passwords, and everything here is read by the API
# process itself. `docker compose up` needs nothing from this file — every variable below
# that the stack needs is set in `docker-compose.yml`'s `api` service, and every one it
# leaves unset is one whose absence is a stated condition there, not an oversight.
#
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET IS NOT HERE AND IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. It is the one variable with no
# default anywhere, it is a signing key, and the repository-root `.env.example` is where it
# is documented along with the command that generates one (ADR-0051). Its boot refusal
# points there rather than here. It is required unconditionally — at least 32 characters
# and not better-auth's published constant — and the values below are what an API booted
# with it also needs.
#
# TASK-003 created this file with the two auth bindings its own boot assertions read.
# TASK-009 (wave 4) added the rest: the two database DSNs, the four trust declarations, the
# build commit and the port. Every variable the API reads is here; a variable the code reads
# that is absent from this file is a defect in this file.
#
# NONE OF THESE IS KEYED ON `NODE_ENV`, and none may be (GC-B, F-386). The image `docker
# compose` runs sets `NODE_ENV=production` unconditionally, so a check gated on it would
# refuse to boot `api` on a laptop; every binding keys on a declared variable instead.
# `NODE_ENV` and `LOG_LEVEL` are read by the logger only — the `env` field on every line
# and the level — and neither decides behaviour.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE. Two DSNs, two roles, and no fallback from one to the other.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The runtime role, `shortkit_app`. It owns nothing, cannot run DDL, and holds no
# BYPASSRLS — boot refuses if it does (ADR-0003). Every tenant-scoped statement the API
# issues goes through this pool. Required; boot refuses without it. The value below is the
# compose stack's database as reached FROM THE HOST, on the published port 55432; inside
# the stack the host is `postgres:5432` and `docker-compose.yml` sets that form.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://shortkit_app:app@127.0.0.1:55432/shortkit

# Better Auth's role, `shortkit_auth`, and the ONLY role holding privileges on its five
# tables — migration 0001 revokes `shortkit_app` on all of them (ADR-0050). Required, and
# there is deliberately no fallback to DATABASE_URL: a fallback would restore `shortkit_app`
# as the auth role silently, with every gate still green. If sign-in fails after migration
# 0001, this is the variable to look at, not the REVOKE. Never `shortkit_app` here.
DATABASE_AUTH_URL=postgres://shortkit_auth:auth@127.0.0.1:55432/shortkit

# NOT read by the API. `DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL` is the migrator's DSN — role
# `shortkit_migrator`, which owns the tables — read by `pnpm --filter @shortkit/api
# db:migrate` and by nothing that serves a request (ADR-0033). It is listed so the three
# roles are named in one place, and commented out so a copy of this file cannot hand the
# serving process the role that can DROP everything.
#
# DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgres://shortkit_migrator:migrator@127.0.0.1:55432/shortkit

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH BINDINGS. The origin and the trusted origins; the secret is documented at the root.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The origin this API issues and verifies tokens for. It becomes `baseURL`, the `iss` and
# `aud` claims on every minted token, and — through its SCHEME — whether the session cookie
# carries `Secure`. Unset, better-auth derives all of them from the incoming request's Host
# header and from NODE_ENV, and boot refuses rather than letting it.
#
# `https:` is accepted for any host. `http:` is accepted ONLY for `localhost`, an address in
# 127.0.0.0/8, or `[::1]`. Behind a proxy that terminates TLS, the correct value is the
# PUBLIC `https:` origin — that is what the browser sees and what `iss`, `aud` and the
# cookie's `Secure` flag have to describe.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Comma-separated origins the dashboard is served from. It EXTENDS the API's own origin
# rather than replacing it, so leaving it unset is legal: it costs a browser flow a
# `403 INVALID_ORIGIN` and costs the test suite nothing.
#
# A bare `*` is refused at boot, and so is any entry with a host label that is entirely
# wildcard — `https://*.vercel.app` trusts every application on the platform. A wildcard
# INSIDE a label is fine and is the documented preview form:
# `https://shortkit-*.vercel.app`. The metacharacters are `*` and `?`, and `?` matches one
# character.
WEB_APP_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUST DECLARATIONS. Two boundaries, four variables, and the two boundaries VARY
# INDEPENDENTLY. Read this block whole before setting any one of them (ADR-0040).
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# | Variable                 | Values           | Unset means  | Required when               |
# |--------------------------|------------------|--------------|-----------------------------|
# | CLIENT_TRUST_BOUNDARY    | proxy or direct  | direct       | never; validity is always   |
# | TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER | a header name    | nothing read | CLIENT_TRUST_BOUNDARY=proxy |
# | BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY       | bff or direct    | direct       | never; validity is always   |
# | BFF_PROXY_SECRET         | non-empty string | nothing read | BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY=bff      |
#
# The shape both boundaries share, learned once and applied twice:
#   - VALIDITY IS ASSERTED UNCONDITIONALLY. `Proxy`, `true`, `prod`, `1`, ` proxy` and
#     `PROXY` all refuse boot — in tests, in CI and in compose. `direct` is the permissive
#     branch and a typo must never silently mean the permissive thing. Exact match, no
#     trimming, no case folding. Empty is the same statement as unset.
#   - THE REQUIREMENT IS CONDITIONAL on the named hop. Unset and `direct` assert nothing.
#   - THE BOUNDARY NEVER GOVERNS THE READ. The address read keys on
#     TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER alone; the BFF read keys on BFF_PROXY_SECRET alone. The
#     boundary only decides whether FORGETTING the companion variable is a boot error.
#
# WHAT THE TWO BOUNDARIES MEAN — the names do not say it, and setting one and assuming the
# other followed is the failure this block exists to prevent:
#
#   CLIENT_TRUST_BOUNDARY declares that a hop IN FRONT OF THIS PROCESS — a load balancer, a
#   reverse proxy, a CDN — terminates client connections and sets AND STRIPS one header
#   carrying the client's address. Not "there is a proxy somewhere".
#
#   BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY declares that OUR OWN FRONTEND (apps/web's BFF route) forwards a
#   browser address it authenticates with a shared secret. An API reachable at its own origin
#   with the web app on Vercel proxying to it is `direct` for the first question and `bff`
#   for the second, and it is the deployment where the secret is the ONLY source of a
#   rate-limit principal — which is why the two were not collapsed into one variable.
#
# WHAT DECLARING NEITHER COSTS, stated rather than hidden: no assertion fires, no IP-keyed
# principal is established, the IP-keyed rate buckets do not bind, and
# `trusted_client_ip_unresolved_total` is nonzero from the first request. `authBodyCap` at
# 32 KiB and the email-keyed bucket still bind. This is the compose stack's state, on
# purpose (docker-compose.yml, the block above `api`'s ports).
#
# WHAT NEITHER ASSERTION CAN CHECK: that the declared header is actually stripped by the
# hop, or that the secret matches the frontend's copy. `bff_proxy_auth_mismatch_total` and
# `trusted_client_ip_unresolved_total` are the signals for both.

# `proxy` when a hop in front terminates connections and strips the header below; `direct`
# or unset when clients reach this process directly. Any other value refuses boot.
#
# CLIENT_TRUST_BOUNDARY=

# The header the hop sets and strips: lowercase, `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,63}$`, and NEVER
# `x-forwarded-for` or `forwarded` — both are defined to be appended to rather than
# replaced, so no hop can strip-and-set them, and naming either is refused at boot. Required
# under `proxy`. Read only when set: a request carrying a plausible header under an
# undeclared name is a client choosing its own principal and gets nothing. Its value must be
# a single IP address — a list, a repeated header or a comma is read as absent.
#
# TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=

# `bff` when apps/web's BFF route forwards `x-shortkit-client-ip` authenticated by
# `x-shortkit-proxy-auth`; `direct` or unset otherwise. Any other value refuses boot.
#
# BFF_TRUST_BOUNDARY=

# The shared secret, the same value on both sides — here and in apps/web's server-only
# environment. Required under `bff`; here it is asserted set and non-empty, nothing more.
# The base64url-and-32-characters shape is enforced on the web side by `assert:no-secrets`
# (apps/web/.env.example carries the generation command), and a value that satisfies that
# side satisfies this one. Compared in constant time; a mismatch is ignored and counted,
# never rejected, so probing reveals nothing. Never logged, on either side.
#
# BFF_PROXY_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROCESS. What the process reports and where it listens.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The full 40-character lowercase hex SHA of the commit that was built, reported at
# GET /health. Required; there is no fallback and no `unknown` (ADR-0027) — a build that
# does not know its commit refuses to serve rather than lying about its identity. In an
# image it is baked in as a build argument (`Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`), so a
# container never reads it from here. Set it when running `dist/main.js` on the host:
#
#   GIT_COMMIT_SHA="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
#
# GIT_COMMIT_SHA=

# The TCP port to listen on. Optional: unset, empty, non-integer or out of range falls
# back to 3001, which is what `docker-compose.yml` publishes and probes. Not asserted.
#
# PORT=3001
